## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates design of a scalable, non-destructive editing pipeline, leveraging Swift concurrency, Core Image, and memory-aware rendering. Also tests undo/redo, testability, and accessibility considerations in a production-like feature.

## Key Concepts
- Swift concurrency and actors for safe state sharing
- Core Image for GPU-accelerated filters
- Non-destructive editing via composable filter passes
- Undo/redo through graph state snapshots
- Memory management with tile-based rendering and lazy recomputation
- Testability: unit tests for graph evaluation; integration tests for export
- Accessibility hooks for UI feedback

## Code Example
```swift
// Skeleton: Filter protocol and graph actor
actor FilterGraph {
  struct Node { var id: String; var filter: CIFilter }
  var nodes: [Node] = []
  func applyAll(base: CIImage) async -> CIImage {
    var current = base
    for n in nodes {
      n.filter.setValue(current, forKey: kCIInputImageKey)
      if let out = n.filter.outputImage {
        current = out
      }
    }
    return current
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to handle very large images on memory-constrained devices?
- What trade-offs exist between tile-based rendering vs full-image compositing, and how would you test them?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to design a practical beginner iOS app using Core Data, SwiftUI, and a simple sync workflow while keeping accessibility in mind.

## Key Concepts
- Core Data schema for the Task entity
- MVVM pattern with SwiftUI
- Offline-first architecture with batched sync
- Accessibility labeling in SwiftUI

## Code Example
```swift
// Core Data Task entity
class Task: NSManagedObject {
  @NSManaged var id: UUID
  @NSManaged var title: String
  @NSManaged var dueDate: Date?
  @NSManaged var priority: String
  @NSManaged var tag: String?
  @NSManaged var isSynced: Bool
}
```

```swift
// TaskListView with accessibility
struct TaskListView: View {
  @FetchRequest(sortDescriptors: [SortDescriptor(\"dueDate\")]) 
  private var tasks: FetchedResults<Task>

  var body: some View {
    List(tasks) { task in
      HStack {
        Circle()
          .fill(priorityColor(task.priority))
          .frame(width: 12, height: 12)

        VStack(alignment: .leading) {
          Text(task.title ?? "")
            .accessibilityLabel(\"Task: \\(task.title ?? \"\")")

          if let dueDate = task.dueDate {
            Text(dueDate, style: .date)
              .font(.caption)
              .accessibilityLabel(\"Due: \\(dueDate.formatted())")
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  private func priorityColor(_ priority: String) -> Color {
    switch priority {
    case \"high\": return .red
    case \"medium\": return .orange
    case \"low\": return .green
    default: return .gray
    }
  }
}
```

```swift
// Offline-first sync manager
class SyncManager: ObservableObject {
  @Published var isSyncing = false
  @Published var lastSyncError: String?

  func syncPendingChanges() async {
    await MainActor.run { isSyncing = true }

    let pendingTasks = fetchPendingTasks()

    do {
      try await batchSyncToAPI(pendingTasks)
      await MainActor.run { lastSyncError = nil }
    } catch {
      await MainActor.run { lastSyncError = error.localizedDescription }
    }

    await MainActor.run { isSyncing = false }
  }

  private func fetchPendingTasks() -> [Task] { /* fetch logic */ }
  private func batchSyncToAPI(_ tasks: [Task]) async throws { /* mock API call */ }
}
```

